  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 211 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nSunday through Thursday:\n\nThe stalled frontal boundary will be the focus for shower and \nthunderstorm development on Sunday. Exactly where the boundary\nwill be is still in question, but somewhere in Wisconsin. We are\nleaning toward central at this time. Initiation of storms could\nwait for the low level jet to develop in the evening, but it \ncould also go earlier in the afternoon with the convective \ntemperature, subtle mid level shortwave trough, and 850mb warm\nair advection.\n\nModels are showing high CAPE (over 3000 j/kg) and somewhat of a\nstraighter hodograph for southern WI with bulk shear of around \n40 kt. One possibility is that storms initiate in central WI and\nthen dive southeast through southern WI with wind a decent\nthreat. That is only one possibility, because if the storms\ninitiate in southern WI if the boundary is this far south and we\ncan tap into better directional shear, then all hazards will be\na concern. SPC introduced a 15% risk for severe storms Sunday\nAND Monday for southern WI. \n\nWherever the storms lay out a boundary Sunday night could end up\nbeing the focus for additional storms Monday. In addition,\nanother shortwave trough is expected to roll across Lake\nSuperior (or northern WI) Monday afternoon. This could also\ntrigger storms. As long as the environment has time to recover\nbetween Sun evening and Mon afternoon, there will be a severe\nrisk once again. \n\nGenerally speaking, there will be a warm and humid ridge sitting\nover the center of the country through most of next week.\nWisconsin will be on the northern periphery of this ridge, thus\nwarm and humid in southern WI and cooler/less humid in northern\nWI. We will be in the vicinity of the \"ridge riders\" where storm\ncomplexes can develop each evening on the nose of the low level\njet. There is not guarantee that we will see storms every day,\nbut the risk is certainly there for much of the week. \n\nCronce",
